From: memstat 1.2 OHOS adaptation
Subject: [PATCH] memstat: make the /proc scanner portable to the OHOS (musl) libc

memstat 1.2 was written against glibc.  Three constructs break when the
same source is compiled against the musl-based OHOS libc:

1. PATH_MAX sizes the /proc/<pid>/maps line buffer but <limits.h> is
   never included, so it is only reachable on glibc where another header
   pulls it in transitively.  On OHOS the declaration fails and every
   later use of `buff` cascades into "undeclared identifier".

2. sscanf() is given "%4s" writing into perm[4].  A 4-character field
   plus the terminating NUL needs 5 bytes, so this is a one-byte
   out-of-bounds write that the OHOS toolchain reports as
   -Wfortify-source.  Size the buffer to match the field width.

3. read_proc() treats "errno != 0" after strtol(ent->d_name, ...) as a
   fatal parse error.  musl's strtol() sets errno to EINVAL when the
   string holds no digits at all, so the first readdir() entry (".")
   aborts the whole scan with "strtol: Invalid argument" and exit 1 --
   memstat cannot report anything at all on OHOS.  glibc leaves errno
   untouched in that case, which is why the bug is invisible upstream.
   Distinguish "no conversion happened" (endptr still at the start,
   i.e. a non-numeric /proc entry such as ".", "..", "self", "sys")
   from a genuine numeric overflow.

No platform macros are introduced; the changes are plain C and also
compile and behave identically against glibc.

diff --git a/memstat.c b/memstat.c
index 5039fb0..873caed 100644
--- a/memstat.c
+++ b/memstat.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <getopt.h>
 #include <errno.h>
+#include <limits.h>

 /* blacklist devices that just map physical memory */
 char *blacklist[] = {
@@ -64,7 +65,7 @@ static void read_proc(void)
 {
     unsigned int nread, pid;
     unsigned long inode, lo, hi, offs;
-    char *p, major[8], minor[8], buff[PATH_MAX + 300], *path, perm[4];
+    char *p, major[8], minor[8], buff[PATH_MAX + 300], *endp, *path, perm[5];
     DIR *d;
     struct dirent *ent;
     FILE *f;
@@ -80,8 +81,11 @@ static void read_proc(void)
 	if (ent == NULL)
 	    break;
 	errno = 0;
-	pid = strtol(ent->d_name, NULL, 10);
-	if (errno != 0) {
+	pid = strtol(ent->d_name, &endp, 10);
+	if (endp == ent->d_name) {	/* not a pid directory */
+		continue;
+	}
+	if (errno != 0) {		/* numeric overflow */
 		perror("strtol");
 		exit(1);
 	}
